      {
        "id": "def-finite-discrete-memoryless-relay-channel-and-causal-code",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Finite discrete memoryless relay channel and causal code",
        "deps": [
          "def-finite-discrete-memoryless-network-and-code"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "statement": "A **finite discrete memoryless relay channel** is the three-node instance of [[def-finite-discrete-memoryless-network-and-code]] with source input $X\\in\\mathcal X$, relay input $X_r\\in\\mathcal X_r$, relay observation $Y_r\\in\\mathcal Y_r$, destination observation $Y\\in\\mathcal Y$, and transition law $W(y_r,y\\mid x,x_r)$. For a positive integer message-set size $M$, the one-way message is uniform on $[M]$ and the destination estimates it from $Y^n$.\n\nIn the **relay-only causal model**, $X_i=f_i(M)$ and $X_{r,i}=g_i(Y_r^{i-1})$. In the **full output-feedback model**, both encoders know the past pair $(Y^{i-1},Y_r^{i-1})$, so $X_i=f_i(M,Y^{i-1},Y_r^{i-1})$ and $X_{r,i}=g_i(Y^{i-1},Y_r^{i-1})$. In either model the channel outputs at time $i$ are drawn from $W(\\cdot,\\cdot\\mid X_i,X_{r,i})$; no encoder sees a current-time output before choosing its current input. The rate is $n^{-1}\\ln M$, and achievability uses average error under the uniform message law."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-finite-two-user-interference-channel-and-strong-regime",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Finite two-user interference channel and strong-interference regime",
        "deps": [
          "def-finite-discrete-memoryless-network-and-code"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "statement": "A **finite two-user interference channel** is the two-sender, two-receiver memoryless channel with finite nonempty input alphabets $\\mathcal X_1,\\mathcal X_2$, finite nonempty output alphabets $\\mathcal Y_1,\\mathcal Y_2$, and transition law $W(y_1,y_2|x_1,x_2)$. Independent uniform messages $M_1,M_2$ on finite nonempty sets are encoded separately as $X_1^n=f_1(M_1)$ and $X_2^n=f_2(M_2)$; receiver $i$ estimates $M_i$ from $Y_i^n$. The **strong-interference regime** means that, for every product law $P_{X_1}P_{X_2}$,\n$$I(X_1;Y_1|X_2)\\le I(X_1;Y_2|X_2),\\qquad I(X_2;Y_2|X_1)\\le I(X_2;Y_1|X_1).$$\nThe inequalities are evaluated under the stated product law and the channel. They are universal hypotheses over input laws, not just inequalities at a chosen optimizing distribution."
      },
